  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The labour inspection system modernized and strengthened towards a more effective prevention-oriented role</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon faces weak labour market governance, high informality and limited enforcement capacity, increasing risks of child labour, hazardous work and rights violations, particularly among vulnerable workers, refugees and host communities. The ILO supports strengthened labour administration and protection systems through targeted action on child labour, occupational safety and health (OSH), and labour law reform.

During the biennium, the intervention will contribute to more preventive, coordinated and inclusive systems for protecting children and workers. It will support the revision of the National Action Plan on Child Labour Reduction, strengthen stakeholder knowledge and coordination at national and area levels, and promote awareness of the worst forms of child labour. In agriculture, it will advance safer working conditions by supporting the validation and roll-out of an OSH guide, including training for extension staff and farmers.

The intervention also supports ongoing labour law reform through technical assistance and policy dialogue to strengthen alignment with fundamental principles and rights at work, including freedom of association, collective bargaining, non-discrimination, OSH and protection at work. Once the revised draft labour law is finalized, support will focus on related policies and implementation frameworks to promote effective application in line with international labour standards.

Beneficiaries include workers, children at risk of child labour, farmers, refugees and host communities, as well as government institutions and social partners. The strategy is implemented with tripartite constituents and relevant national institutions.</narrative>
  </description>
